(*
porta space-sync — the bridge's pending queue is BOUNDED.

Found on hardware (plan 3, Task 5): with the gateway unreachable, `applyAck:` never
runs, `pending` grows on every captured stream write, and the node OOMs and panics
at about ninety seconds on a 128 KB heap. A miss must cost latency and BOUNDED
loss, never unbounded memory.

The loss is deliberate and it is counted: dropping silently would make a node that
threw away telemetry indistinguishable from one that had none to send.

Oldest-first, because for tel/** the newest entry is the one worth keeping — and it
keeps the surviving rows in ascending seq order, which the push frame requires.

The exact invariant is `dropped + pendingSize == n` — every write is either kept or
counted, never both and never neither — so the assertions below check EQUALITY
(`pendingSize == cap`, `dropped == n - cap`), not the looser `dropped >= (n - cap)`
that would still pass if some writes vanished uncounted.

socket: nil is the whole test rig: no send, no receive, so no ack, forever.
expect:
pending starts 0
pending size exact true
dropped exact true
newest kept true
oldest evicted true
*)
class PendingBound packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bridge cap n last rows sawOld spans |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: nil space: space id: 'n1' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans timers: platform timers.
    bridge subscribeStream: 'tel/log'.

    ('pending starts ' , bridge pendingSize printString) out.

    (* Write far more than the cap. 4x is enough to prove a bound without making
       the test slow. `drain` runs inline: `out:` only enqueues onto the space's
       own fixed-depth ring (OEVM_SPACE_QUEUE_DEPTH, space.h) — undrained, THAT
       ring overruns and sheds posts long before PortaBridge ever sees them,
       which would prove the wrong bound. Draining every write is what a real
       check-in tick does (the node's own `report`/`checkIn` loop calls `space
       drain` every tick), so this is the reaction actually running, not a queue
       starving upstream of it. *)
    cap:: bridge maxPending.
    n:: cap * 4.
    1 to: n do: [ :i | space out: 'tel/log' value: i. space drain ].

    ('pending size exact ' , (bridge pendingSize = cap) printString) out.
    ('dropped exact ' , (bridge dropped = (n - cap)) printString) out.

    (* The newest write must have survived, and the very first must not have. Read
       the queue through the push frame the bridge would actually send: rows carry
       {seq, path, kind, value} and the value is what `space out:` wrote. *)
    rows:: bridge pendingRows.
    last:: (rows at: rows size) at: 4.
    ('newest kept ' , (last = n) printString) out.
    sawOld:: false.
    1 to: rows size do: [ :i | ((rows at: i) at: 4) = 1 ifTrue: [ sawOld:: true ] ].
    ('oldest evicted ' , sawOld not printString) out.
    ^ 0
  )
) : (
  public packageUsing: manifest = ( ^ self new )
)
